// Core pointer primitive surface.
//
// `Ptr` is not intended to be ordinary application-facing data. It names the
// low-level compiler/runtime boundary that safe core views such as `Slice`,
// `Array`, `Vec`, and future text views lower through after proof checking.
//
// Public core types expose access contracts over authority-bearing views and
// owners. A raw pointer remains inert even when a calling plan describes its
// native representation; it is never itself a read or write capability.

// Opaque, provider-minted carriers. Their representation and construction stay
// below the boundary: naming `Ptr<T>` or `PtrRange<T>` does not let ordinary
// source manufacture an address, provenance, bounds, or access authority.
boundary data Ptr<T>;
boundary data PtrRange<T>;

boundary operator Ptr::offset<T>(ptr: Ptr<T>, count: u64) -> Ptr<T>;

boundary operator Ptr::range<T>(ptr: Ptr<T>, len: u64) -> PtrRange<T>;
